              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Men have larger, more prominent Adam\u2019s apples primarily due to the effects of **testosterone during puberty** and the resulting **anatomical shape** of their throat cartilage. \n\nTo understand why this happens, it helps to look at what the \"Adam\u2019s apple\" actually is and how it develops.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What is the Adam's apple?\nThe medical term for the Adam\u2019s apple is the **laryngeal prominence**. It is not a separate bone or organ; rather, it is a bump formed by the **thyroid cartilage**, a tough but flexible shield of cartilage that wraps around the front of your larynx (voice box) to protect your vocal cords. \n\nEveryone\u2014both males and females\u2014is born with this cartilage, and before puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized throat structures and high-pitched voices.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Role of Testosterone and Puberty\nDuring puberty, boys experience a massive surge of testosterone. This hormone triggers several physical changes (secondary sex characteristics), including the rapid growth of the larynx and the vocal cords. \n* As the larynx grows, the vocal cords stretch and thicken, which causes the voice to drop and become much deeper.\n* Because the larynx inside becomes much larger, the protective shield of thyroid cartilage surrounding it must also grow larger to accommodate it.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Sharpness of the Angle (The Main Visual Difference)\nThe biggest reason the Adam\u2019s apple is highly visible in men but flat in women comes down to the **angle at which the cartilage plates meet**. \n\nThe thyroid cartilage consists of two plates that join together at the front of your throat, forming a V-shaped notch. \n* **In males:** Because of the rapid expansion of the larynx during puberty, these two plates fuse together at a sharp **90-degree angle**. This acute angle creates a distinct, protruding point that juts out noticeably under the skin.\n* **In females:** The plates meet at a much wider, gentler **120-degree angle**. Because the angle is so wide, the cartilage lies relatively flat against the front of the neck, making it much less noticeable.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Fat Distribution\nAdditionally, sex-specific fat distribution plays a minor role. Adult females naturally tend to store slightly more subcutaneous (under-the-skin) fat around the neck area. This extra layer of tissue acts as a natural cushion that helps smooth out the contours of the neck, further obscuring the underlying thyroid cartilage.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, men have more prominent Adam's apples because testosterone makes their voice box grow larger. To protect this larger voice box, the cartilage plates of the throat fuse together at a sharp 90-degree angle, causing them to stick out, whereas in women, the plates meet at a flatter, wider 120-degree angle.",
